      "problem": "Given the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$, find $‖4v‖.$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that we can factor a constant out of the norm, provided that we take the absolute value of the constant:\n\n$$\n‖kv‖=|k|\\cdot ‖v‖\n$$\n\nUsing this fact and the formula for the norm, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖4v‖ & =|4|\\cdot ‖v‖ \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{{v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{1+9+1+9} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
